;; ehrt.sim's resource theory -- the WANT (the ultimately
;; envisioned system; our best current guess, expected to change).
;; docs/sim-theory.md is the prose companion and defines the
;; now/next/want reading. Notation is ehr-testing-tools'
;; docs/notation.md (equation form, five stage kinds, catalytic
;; targets, unions, external stages); this file keeps that repo's
;; pipeline.edn schema shape exactly, so its equation->diagram
;; machinery consumes this file unchanged.
;;
;; now/next/want convention: the WANT is this whole file. The NOW is
;; derivable as the :status :built subset. The NEXT is the single
;; stage marked ";; NEXT" below. No extra schema keys are invented
;; for this -- the convention lives in comments and the companion
;; prose, keeping the file loadable by tools' Pipeline Malli as-is.
{:schema-version 1
 :stages
 [;; Milestone M4 (.agents/plans/roadmap.md): Persona landed for real --
  ;; demographics sampling from vendored, hashed tables, plus payer-pool
  ;; (the catalytic this comment used to name as a forward reference) now
  ;; a real wire. M3's own NEXT (Execute's order/result step family)
  ;; landed the session before -- see :execute's own :contract for that
  ;; record.
  {:id :persona
   :label "Persona"
   :kind :transform
   :status :built
   :inputs ["sim-config"]
   :outputs ["persona"]
   :catalytic ["demographics-tables" "payer-pool"]
   :laws ["samples only from the run's single seeded RNG, in fixed order (global determinism law, docs/sim-theory.md) -- property-tested (persona-test/persona-consumes-a-fixed-number-of-rng-draws-regardless-of-content, 100 trials green 2026-07-26): 13 draws, always, regardless of which pool/decade-bucket any weighted pick lands in"
          "US-centric by data, not by code: nationality lives entirely in demographics-tables (catalytic target 3), never in stage logic"]
   :contract "v1 scope (M4, ehrt.sim.persona): name, DOB (age from a configured uniform distribution), sex, address, US-format phone, an SSN-shaped identifier (fixed 900 area-number prefix, SSA's own permanently-never-issued block -- notes/facts-register.md F8, the same obviously-synthetic-by-construction design move ADR-0007 already made for provider NPIs), and payer (age-linked, Medicare dominant 65+, docs/operational-models.md) -- ALL sampled together, once, at patient creation. IMPLEMENTATION NOTE, kept here so the diagram stays truthful about topology while being honest about code shape: this stage's equation (sim-config -> persona) is satisfied by an engine-internal `:registered` event ehrt.sim-engine.engine/run prepends to EVERY patient's step queue (never authorable pathway IR, the same treatment :result-followup already gets) -- persona is folded into Execute's own step-queue mechanism rather than a structurally separate pipeline pass, because a patient's persona is consumed at the same init moment Execute already owns (arrival scheduling), not by a downstream stage that exists yet (RunModules, :planned, is the eventual real consumer of `persona` as a wire resource). This stage remains its own equation entry -- not folded into :execute's own contract -- because it has its own laws (fixed-draw-count, US-centric-by-data) worth stating independently of Execute's. Vendored tables (resources/demographics/) are SMALL and HAND-CURATED, not extracted from Synthea -- resources/demographics/NOTICE records why (no ../ checkout was available this session), in the same schema shape a real extraction would use."}

  {:id :trajectory
   :label "RunModules"
   :kind :transform
   :status :built
   :inputs ["persona"]
   :outputs ["clinical-trajectory"]
   :catalytic ["gmf-module-set" "gmf-interpreter"]
   :laws ["code passthrough: every coded concept ({:system :code :display}) in a trajectory event is carried verbatim from its source module -- the interpreter never invents or translates codes"
          "glass-box traceability: every trajectory event cites the module id and state name that produced it, so any output event is auditable back to inspectable module JSON (validation cross-cutting argument, docs/problem-statement.md)"]
   :contract "v1 semantics: docs/gmf-interpreter.md is this stage's design doc -- the state-type/transition subset it executes (deferring CallSubmodule/Counter/MultiObservation/Death), the history/horizon two-phase run per patient, the encounter-class mapping, and the module-namespaced :attributes discipline. gmf-module-set's catalytic target is RESOLVED (ADR-0013, author-ratified 2026-07-27): target 3, a small curated hashed subset vendored into resources/modules/, not target 1 (artifacts.lock). M5a (landed): the interpreter LIBRARY itself -- ehrt.sim-trajectory.gmf (the loader) and ehrt.sim-trajectory.gmf-interpreter (step/walk-module/run-module, the history/horizon two-phase run) -- tested end to end against docs/gmf-interpreter.md's own hand-written fixture (ADR-0013 point 6). M5b (landed): the FIRST real vendored module (sinusitis.json, resources/modules/NOTICE) plus the actual persona -> run-module wiring inside ehrt.sim-engine.engine/run's own :registered decide method (module assignment, ehrt.sim-engine.engine/assign-module, composes with :pathways -- both just IR entering the pathway-ir union, below) -- this is the flip docs/gmf-interpreter.md section 7 named as M5b's own job, not M5a's"}

  {:id :compile
   :label "CompileTrajectory"
   :kind :transform
   :status :built
   :inputs ["clinical-trajectory"]
   :outputs ["compiled-pathway"]
   :laws ["clinical-content-preserving: every clinical trajectory event maps to at least one IR step; none is dropped, reordered against clinical causality, or invented"
          "appends provenance: each compiled step cites the trajectory event it realizes"]
   :contract "the novel layer -- neither mined source has this stage (Simulated Hospital has no trajectory generation; Synthea has no operational compilation), so its laws carry fresh validation burden rather than inherited provenance (docs/problem-statement.md, Cross-Cutting Arguments). M5b (landed, ehrt.sim-trajectory.compile-trajectory): docs/gmf-interpreter.md Sec 1's per-state-type table is realized exactly -- :procedure/:observation/:medication-order/:medication-end each a new standalone IR step (:citation carrying the module-state provenance); ConditionOnset/ConditionEnd an ANNOTATION on the enclosing Encounter-mapped step's own :conditions vector, never a step of their own; Encounter/EncounterEnd map to :outpatient-visit(-end) or :admission/:discharge per encounter class (Sec 4). Pre-horizon ConditionOnset/ConditionEnd/MedicationOrder/MedicationEnd become REGISTRATION-TIME facts (ratified item 5) riding the engine's own :registered event; pre-horizon Encounter/Procedure/Observation are dropped (no operational trajectory event for the encounter machinery during history, Sec 3). One vendored module's own recurring-for-life design (sinusitis.json's Potential_Onset loop) surfaced a real scope boundary this stage now enforces: only the FIRST horizon-phase encounter compiles into IR, per this project's own single-encounter-horizon law (ADR-0007 point 3) -- everything after is out of scope, a fact the underlying (uncompiled) clinical-trajectory still carries in full. The day -> minutes conversion (docs/patient-state-model.md's durations rule, extended) happens at exactly one point here, bridging gaps between compiled steps with a deterministic :delay."}

  {:id :churn
   :label "InjectChurn"
   :kind :transform
   :status :built
   :inputs ["pathway-ir" "churn-profile"]
   :outputs ["operational-pathway"]
   :laws ["IR endomorphism: operational-pathway is itself valid pathway IR -- churn injection is IR -> IR, so the engine needs no knowledge that churn exists -- property-tested (churn-test/inject-always-produces-valid-ir, 100 trials green 2026-07-26)"
          "clinical-steps invariant: only inserts operational-noise steps (cancel-admit/cancel-transfer/cancel-discharge, transfer-in-error, bed-swap, merge); never removes, reorders, or alters clinical steps -- property-tested as its own converse (churn-test/strip-of-inject-recovers-the-input-for-any-profile-and-seed, 100 trials green 2026-07-26): stripping every churn-insertable step type back out of a churned pathway recovers the original exactly"
          "all stochastic choices draw from the run's single seeded RNG: `inject` takes engine/run's OWN java.util.Random, never a derived/isolated stream (ADR-0009's own NPI-generation reasoning, extended)"
          "zero-probability identity: an all-zero churn-profile is the identity transform, for ANY seed -- property-tested (churn-test/zero-profile-is-the-identity-transform-for-any-seed, 100 trials green 2026-07-26), not merely assumed from the RNG-consumption law"]
   :contract "v1 scope (M2b, docs/patient-state-model.md's event-validity table as InjectChurn's applicability oracle, ehrt.sim-engine.churn): six step types (cancel-admit/cancel-transfer/cancel-discharge -> A11/A12/A13, transfer-in-error -> A02+A12, bed-swap -> A17, merge -> A40), each co-landed with check.clj invariants and message-type-registry entries in the same change (AGENTS.md's co-landing convention, extended to this stage). Applicability is tracked as a small type-level state machine over the pathway's own step sequence (no facility/occupancy visibility at injection time) -- decide still independently re-validates against LIVE world state at execution time and rejects (not throws) when a statically-legal insertion turns out to collide with something only runtime knows about (e.g. a bed a cancel-discharge would reinstate into has since been reclaimed); a rejected step is a no-op, not a run-halting condition. Wired into ehrt.sim.run/run-command via `:churn` (a modest built-in sample-profile) or an explicit `:churn-profile` map. The WANT is InjectChurn choosing insertion points from real operational realism data (site profiles, Calibrate feedback) rather than flat per-gap probabilities -- not this session's scope."}

  {:id :execute
   :label "Execute"
   :kind :transform
   :status :built
   :inputs ["operational-pathway"]
   :outputs ["ground-truth-log" "state-history"]
   :catalytic ["provider-pool" "order-profiles"]
   :laws ["determinism: single seeded RNG, totally ordered event queue ([t seq-no] tiebreak) => byte-identical serialized output for identical inputs+seed -- property-tested (engine-test/determinism-holds-for-all-seeds, 100 trials green 2026-07-26)"
          "decide/evolve split (ADR-0008): decide (rng, t, world, mrn, step) -> {:events :advance} consults world-so-far and the RNG but never mutates; evolve (world, event) -> world' is the ONLY function that changes patient state, pure and total, folding one event at a time -- so state-history is DERIVED (reduce evolve over a log prefix), not a second output the engine must keep in sync with the log by discipline; this is what makes state-based emitters renderings rather than redesigns (ADR-0002)"
          "order-profiles catalytic (target 3, hashed US-units config, docs/operational-models.md; LANDED M3): :order/:result-followup step types under Execute's existing :built contract, an EmitHL7-style co-landing addition, not a new stage -- repairs the plan's earlier capture gap named at this catalytic's first mention (order profiles and the ORM/ORU cycle were discussed from the first session but appeared in no planning artifact before M3's own roadmap entry). Auto-pairing law: an :order's decide call samples its :result-available event FULLY, atomically, at order-time (all RNG draws happen in that one call, the same 'decided atomically' precedent :transfer-in-error already set) but the result event rides a NEW `:schedule-followup` decide-outcome key rather than being spliced into :events directly -- splicing a future-t event into the current call's :events would enter ground-truth at THIS call's own log position, ahead of other patients' smaller-:t events the run loop hasn't processed yet, breaking the log's own global t-ordering; `:schedule-followup` instead asks the run loop to enqueue a genuine future [t seq-no] entry, the same queue every other scheduled event already goes through. Computed-truth mini-law: a result's abnormal flag is DERIVED from value vs reference-range (ehrt.sim-engine.order-profiles/abnormal-flag), never sampled independently -- property-tested (order-profiles-test/abnormal-flag-agrees-with-sample-analyte-value-for-any-draw, 200 trials green 2026-07-26)"
          "provider-pool catalytic (target 3, hashed config, docs/operational-models.md): attending assignment at admission draws from the provider pool the same way bed assignment draws from facility config -- both are Execute-time allocations, not separate stages"]
   :contract "v1 scope (Milestone M1, docs/operational-models.md): the walking-skeleton step set (:admission :delay :discharge) plus :transfer, engine-assigned via the allocation ladder and provider sampling -- provider-pool is a real catalytic now (synthetic Luhn-valid NPIs materialized once per run, ehrt.sim.config), not just a declared wire. M2a (ADR-0010, ADR-0011): patient-id is the fold/queue key, :mrn moved into state as {:mrns :active-mrn}, every event carries :participants, the clock is seconds (pathway IR's :delay stays minutes, converted at decide-time), and a configurable warm-up window marks early events. M2b: the churn family (cancel-admit/cancel-transfer/cancel-discharge/transfer-in-error/bed-swap/merge, landed as :execute step types the SAME way :transfer was -- :churn, above, is what decides WHERE to insert them into a pathway, this stage is what runs them) plus result-not-throw capacity-exhaustion (a structured {:exhausted ...} outcome, never a thrown exception, when the allocation ladder is fully exhausted). M3 (docs/operational-models.md's order-profiles catalytic, this session): :order (author-facing IR step, `{:type :order :profile :cbc}`) and the engine-internal :result-followup (never hand-authored -- an :order's own decide call auto-pairs it, keeping authored pathways ergonomic, the choice recorded over a hand-authored :result{:order-ref}} step, docs/patient-state-model.md); a run's `world` gains :order-profiles (default ehrt.sim-engine.order-profiles/default-profiles, a small hand-curated CBC+BMP starter set, real LOINC codes verified against loinc.org -- notes/facts-register.md F7). The run loop itself gained ONE new mechanism for this: decide's outcome may carry :schedule-followup, a genuine future [t seq-no] queue entry (not a splice into the current call's :events) -- see :execute's own laws above for why. All of this landed under this SAME :built contract, no new input/output, one new catalytic wire (order-profiles, already declared, now real -- docs/sim-theory.md records why this needed no diagram regeneration). The WANT is the full Simulated-Hospital-derived step vocabulary, landed step by step under the co-landing law (see :check)"}

  {:id :check
   :label "Check"
   :kind :judge
   :status :built
   :inputs ["ground-truth-log"]
   :outputs ["pass" "rejected"]
   :catalytic ["invariant-catalog"]
   :laws ["never modifies the log it judges (judge kind's own floor)"
          "co-landing: every step type the engine executes has invariants in the catalog, added in the same change (AGENTS.md convention) -- a coupling law between :execute and this stage, held by discipline and reviewable by diffing the step set against the catalog"
          "produces no :indeterminate in v0 -- stated explicitly, same as tools' own Check, since :indeterminate is otherwise part of the judge kind's floor"]
   :contract "the catalog doubles as validation claim #3's engine and the regression suite (docs/problem-statement.md); property-tested over randomized runs (engine-test/every-run-satisfies-invariant-catalog, 200 trials green 2026-07-26)"}

  {:id :emit-hl7
   :label "EmitHL7"
   :kind :transform
   :status :built
   :inputs ["ground-truth-log"]
   :outputs ["hl7v2-stream"]
   :catalytic ["hl7-parser-dep" "message-type-registry" "snomed-icd10-map" "site-profile"]
   :laws ["bidirectional derivability: every emitted message is derivable from the log, and every message is traceable back to the log event that produced it (problem-statement Guarantees) -- the property that makes the corpus a test instrument rather than mere traffic -- property-tested (emit-hl7-test/bidirectional-derivability, 100 trials green 2026-07-26)"
          "native code rendering: concept triplets render as coded fields (CWE etc.) in their own system; the ONE sanctioned translation is SNOMED->ICD-10-CM for billing segments (DG1), via the pinned NLM map (catalytic target 1), or code pairs carried from the module where provided"
          "dialect invariance (docs/site-profiles.md, the site-profiles milestone's own thesis): for a fixed (config, seed), ground-truth-log is byte-identical across any two site-profile catalytic values -- site-profile can only change hl7v2-stream's rendering (MSH-3/4/5/6/12, code-table-overridden fields, Z-segment content), never a fact -- property-tested (emit-hl7-test/invariance-messages-agree-after-masking-dialect-surfaces, 100 trials green 2026-07-26) alongside the structural guarantee that :site-profile is not a member of ehrt.sim-engine.engine/config-keys at all"]
   :contract "v0 scope was ADT^A01 (admission) and ADT^A03 (discharge) only; M1 added A02 (transfer); M2b adds the full churn family -- A11/A12/A13 (cancel-admit/cancel-transfer/cancel-discharge), A17 (bed-swap, ONE message carrying both patients' PID/PV1 pairs -- the real HL7v2 A17 shape), and A40 (merge, PID carries the surviving MRN, MRG-1 the prior/merged-away one). MSH/EVN/PID/PV1(/MRG) populated minimally, on org.clojars.cmiles74/clojure-hl7-parser's structures (the emitter's only runtime dependency, docs/third-party-sources.md). Timestamps render from a pinned :reference-date run-config input (ehrt.sim-emit-hl7.emit-hl7/default-reference-date), never wall-clock. M3: ORM^O01 (order-placed -- ORC-1 \"NW\", OBR-4 the panel-level LOINC concept as CWE) and ORU^R01 (result-available -- OBR-4 the same panel concept, one OBX per analyte: OBX-3 CWE with the analyte's own LOINC triplet, OBX-5 value, OBX-6 units, OBX-7 reference range as \"low-high\", OBX-8 the abnormal flag rendered in HL7's own N/L/H vocabulary, Table 0078). Neither carries EVN (an ADT-specific segment, not part of the order-message family, HL7v2 convention). :step-rejected renders NOTHING -- no message-type-registry entry, by design (ADR-0012): truth about the run, never wire traffic. The site-profiles milestone (this session): `site-profile` (ehrt.sim-emit-hl7.site-profile/SiteProfile, catalytic target 3 -- an optional, all-keys-optional config value, absent/nil/{} all rendering identically to the pre-milestone baseline) lands as a REAL catalytic wire, not merely declared -- MSH-3/4/5/6/12 (sending/receiving app+facility, version id -- SimHospital issue #17's own citation, .agents/plans/roadmap.md), PV1-2/PV1-36 (patient-class/discharge-disposition code-table overrides, rendering-time substitutions over the SAME underlying state value), and a Z-segment template DSL (field->state/persona/event-path bindings, rendered after standard segments on their own declared trigger, escaped per ER7, unbound paths empty rather than throwing -- SimHospital issue #21's own citation for the custom-segment scope item). `:naming :surge-format`'s migration to the profile is the one documented exception bound at CONFIG time rather than emit time (ehrt.sim-emit-hl7.site-profile/apply-naming, a facility-config transform a caller applies before Execute, never auto-wired) -- named here so this stage's own catalytic doesn't silently claim credit for it."}

  {:id :emit-state
   :label "EmitState"
   :kind :transform
   :status :built
   :inputs ["state-history"]
   :outputs ["state-document"]
   :laws ["snapshot-at-instant: a state-document is a pure function of state-history at a queried simulated instant -- no access to the log, the engine, or the RNG -- property-tested (v2-replay-test/emitter-coherence-reconstructed-state-matches-the-log-fold-at-every-boundary, 150 trials green, plus its own module-driven-outpatient sibling, 150 trials green, 2026-07-27)"
          "format dispatch: \"state-document\" is FHIR resources or a CDA document, resolved at call time -- one equation for both formats, the same deliberate collapsing tools' Gate documents for judge.fhir/judge.v2 rather than duplicating the equation per format"]
   :contract "M6 (landed): FHIR R4 only (ehrt.sim-emit-fhir.emit-fhir) -- a Bundle (JSON, data.json) of Patient/Encounter/Condition/Observation/MedicationRequest/Coverage, minimal-but-valid, built entirely from ehrt.sim-engine.engine's own folded PatientState (extended this milestone: :conditions/:observations/:medication-orders/:discharged-at now land in the fold itself, the same reason :location/:persona already do -- state-history could not otherwise hold what EmitState needed to render without reading the log directly, which the snapshot-at-instant law forbids). CDA is the format dispatch's OTHER arm, deferred with this contract note -- no XML shell, no half-finished document type, a future session adds a sibling emitter namespace and a dispatch key, not an extension of this one. `sim run --emit fhir [--at <seconds>]` (default: end of run) is the CLI surface, the same verb grammar `--emit hl7` already established. Cross-emitter id sub-law (below): every resource id/reference derives from patient-id/active-mrn, the SAME identifiers EmitHL7 renders -- property-tested (emit-fhir-test/fhir-patient-id-and-active-mrn-resolve-to-the-same-hl7-identity, 150 trials green, 2026-07-27). Sim split B stage M3 (2026-08-04, ADR-0043): the namespace above moved out of `components/sim` into its own component, `components/sim-emit-fhir`, sim-emit-hl7's own sibling."}

  {:id :package
   :label "Package"
   :kind :transform
   :status :planned
   :inputs ["hl7v2-stream" "ground-truth-log"]
   :outputs ["sim-corpus" "run-manifest"]
   :laws ["appends provenance: the manifest records every pinned input (seed, engine params, config hash, environment) in the tools-ingestible ManifestV1_1 mirror shape (ADR-0001 clause 5; ehrt.sim.manifest, :built ahead of its stage)"]
   :contract "manifest construction is :built (manifest-test green); packaging around it is :planned -- a stage may be further along than its equation's status when a component shipped early"}

  ;; NEXT -- Milestone M6's own roadmap entry names Calibrate as the
  ;; last planned stage once EmitState lands (this session). Package
  ;; stays :planned too (only its manifest component shipped early,
  ;; its own :contract note above) -- Calibrate is where the marker
  ;; moves per the roadmap's own ordering, not a claim that Package is
  ;; equally far along.
  {:id :calibrate
   :label "Calibrate"
   :kind :feedback
   :status :planned
   :inputs ["sim-corpus" "feed-statistics" "churn-profile"]
   :outputs ["churn-profile"]
   :laws ["round bound: calibration iterates at most a declared N rounds toward the site's observed feed statistics (feedback kind's own floor -- no unbounded loops)"
          "touches only churn-profile parameters; clinical module content is never calibrated against a site feed (that would launder real-feed information into clinical content -- see the no-PHI global law)"]
   :contract "this is validation claim #5's third arm made mechanical: 'match your hospital' instead of 'trust our defaults' (docs/problem-statement.md)"}

  ;; External stages: dashed boxes, no :kind/:status/:laws -- named
  ;; slots this repo makes no claims about (docs/notation.md).
  {:id :sut :label "SystemUnderTest" :external? true
   :inputs ["hl7v2-stream"] :outputs ["sut-behavior"]}

  {:id :tools-intake :label "ToolsCorpusIntake" :external? true
   :inputs ["sim-corpus"] :outputs ["catalog-entry"]}]

 ;; Union resources (docs/notation.md): pathway-ir is THE union of
 ;; ADR-0002 clause 1, stated algebraically -- hand-authored scripts
 ;; and compiled trajectories are indistinguishable to every stage
 ;; downstream of this merge. authored-pathway enters the diagram as
 ;; an author-supplied input resource with no producing stage inside
 ;; this repo, exactly as tools' foreign-file does.
 :resources
 [{:resource "pathway-ir"
   :union-of ["compiled-pathway" "authored-pathway"]}]}
